    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Use masking tape and cotton balls or other soft materials to wrap an egg. Then, drop it from the roof of your school."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Have you ever done this before? It’s a classic science experiment called the egg drop challenge. Every year, many students around the world conduct it. The goal is to keep the eggs from breaking. But as many students quickly learn, that’s easier said than done. Once the eggs break, teachers explain the physics behind the experiment. One popular conclusion is that eggs sitting "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "vertically",
            "zh": "垂直地"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " are less likely to break than eggs sitting "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "horizontally",
            "zh": "水平地"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But is it true?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Tal Cohen is an engineering professor at the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology",
            "zh": "麻省理工学院"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". After running the egg drop challenge for years, she began to wonder if she had been telling students the right story. To find out, she and her colleagues bought more than 200 eggs and took them into the lab."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "In their experiments, the researchers discovered that eggs that landed on their sides were actually less likely to break. This is because the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "shell",
            "zh": "蛋壳"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " on an egg’s sides is less stiff than on its ends. When a dropped egg hits the ground on its side, the shell can "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "absorb",
            "zh": "吸收"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " some of the impact. This effect isn’t seen with eggs dropped on their ends. “It’s just like the human body,” explained Joseph E. Bonavia, one of the researchers. “Imagine you are falling from a height. If you land with your legs straight, you’ll break your bones. You’ll want to bend your knees, and that’s what the egg is doing.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Cohen’s findings remind us that not everything we’ve been told is true. For this reason, we should keep an open mind and never be afraid to “break” some common wisdom."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
